--- body ---
## Context
PR #1434 added an early exit to `scripts/zotero.py reconcile` when nothing is
staged, next to the existing `no_staging` exit. The /gaze panel on that PR
flagged minor gaps (comment 6194717551):

- `reconcile --apply` with nothing staged returns `checked`, exit 0, without
  validating `.zotero-reconcile.json` or the write credentials, so a broken
  opt-in stays silent until a file is staged.
- Neither early exit writes the `--out` file nor carries the full report keys
  (`bib_files`, `staging_dirs`, `index_fetched`, `actions`); the snippet in
  `skills/zotero/references/backfill.md` then announces an empty report path.
- `test_reconcile_with_nothing_staged_skips_the_index` has no counter-case: a
  staged PDF must still load the index, and `--apply` is uncovered.
- The two early exits build near-identical output (one helper), and the
  reconcile-block helpers in `tests/test_molt_ticket_sweep.py` now serve only
  the zotero backfill test.

## Exit criteria
- [x] `--apply` validates the opt-in file and credentials even with nothing staged
- [x] Both early exits write `--out` when given, with the full report keys
- [x] Test: a staged file loads the index; test: `--apply` with nothing staged and a bad opt-in fails
- [x] One helper builds the early-exit report; reconcile test helpers live with the zotero tests
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Validation of the proposed fix

Focused offline tests pass, including both empty exits, output artifacts,
invalid/missing opt-in, missing read credentials, staged-file index loading,
unchanged no-write replay, a missing write key on the main path (report still
written) and the relocated backfill shell test. An empty apply, like a no-write
replay, needs the opt-in and read credentials only; the write key is resolved
only when there is something to write.
Ruff and git diff --check pass. Full make check has not passed here: automatic
approval review blocked the broad test run because it could contact Zotero.
The ticket stays open pending full validation and review.
